    <span class="cat-badge">Guide &middot; Hanoi Motorbike Rental</span>
    <h1>Motorbike Rental Deposits in Hanoi</h1>
    <p>The deposit conversation is the part of renting a motorbike that travelers think about least and should think about most. It is where the rental stops being a friendly exchange and becomes an agreement, and the difference between a smooth return and a stressful one is almost always decided by what was agreed about the deposit before the key changed hands. This guide explains how deposits work in Hanoi rental generally, what good practice looks like from both sides, and the questions worth asking before you ride.</p>
    <p>One thing this guide does not do is invent our specific terms. Our deposit arrangements are agreed directly with us for each rental, and the honest way to learn them is to ask. What the guide provides is the general shape of the practice in this city, the standards that separate clean rentals from ambiguous ones, and the habits that protect your money wherever you rent. It belongs to the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/">Hanoi motorbike rental</a> hub, alongside the <a href="/hanoi-motorbike-rental/how-to-rent-a-motorbike-in-hanoi/">how to rent in Hanoi</a> guide.</p>

    <nav class="toc" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p>On this page</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#sec-1">Why deposits exist at all</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-2">Cash deposit versus held passport</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-3">What a deposit should cover, and not</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-4">The paperwork that protects both sides</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-5">The return: where deposits are won or lost</a></li>
        <li><a href="#sec-6">The questions to ask before you commit</a></li>
